Yes. **Comparing the 21 pipelines individually, side by side in VS Code, is better than reviewing one consolidated difference file.** It makes it easier to identify filter changes, ignore environment-specific differences and update the configuration without accidentally copying PROD outputs.

You can automate opening all 21 comparisons sequentially instead of manually entering each command.

## **Step 1: Prepare the manual-review files**

You have already updated eight pipelines in `updated-dev-pipelines`. Keep those changes untouched.

Execute:

```
cd ~/lma-pipeline-comparison

mkdir -p manual-review-pipelines

awk -F, '$2=="MANUAL_REVIEW" {print $1}' \
section_comparison.csv > manual-review.txt

while read -r pipeline
do
    cp -n "dev-pipelines/$pipeline.conf" \
    manual-review-pipelines/
done < manual-review.txt
```

Verify the number of pipelines:

```
wc -l manual-review.txt
```

Expected result: **21**.

## **Step 2: Automatically open all 21 comparisons in VS Code**

Copy and paste this entire block into your Mac terminal:

```
VSCODE="/Applications/Visual Studio Code.app/Contents/Resources/app/bin/code"

while read -r pipeline
do
    echo "================================"
    echo "Reviewing: $pipeline"
    echo "================================"

    "$VSCODE" --wait --diff \
    "prod-pipelines/$pipeline.conf" \
    "manual-review-pipelines/$pipeline.conf"

done < manual-review.txt
```

This will open one pipeline comparison at a time. Once you finish reviewing and close the comparison tab using `Command + W`, the next pipeline should open automatically.

